# Target C — Type-II low-q-k boundary-defect tradeoff

**Date:** 2026-09-12  
**Lifecycle:** proof-only Type-II composition; theorem-state authority unchanged.  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** **STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**

This note makes explicit a source-level tradeoff implicit in the cellwise rhombus
payment. It combines the proof-only Type-II loaded-hull localization with the
proof-only Section-132 square-root payment and keeps the exact residual outer-walk
slack.

Put

- k=d_H+s_Z;
- t=(q-k)/D=(h-s_Z)/D;
- Q for the global bounded nonquadrilateral degree defect;
- u_B for the exact loaded-hull outer-walk slack from Section 63; and
- s_C for the total pinned-contact surplus of the unloaded components.

Then every Type-II sequence in the common scope of Sections 125 and 132 satisfies

\[
\boxed{
3(h-s_Z)-2s_C
=(q-k)+2Q+2u_B+2c_I+4,
}
\tag{163.1}
\]

where c_I is the number of unloaded positive-contact components. Since the canonical
component bound gives c_I=O(D^{2/3}), the normalized form is

\[
\boxed{
\frac{3(h-s_Z)-2s_C}{D}
=t+2\frac{Q+u_B}{D}+o(1).
}
\tag{163.2}
\]

The exact Section-132 lower payment therefore implies the tradeoff

\[
\boxed{
\liminf\frac{Q+u_B}{D}
\ge
\frac12\left(2\sqrt{\Delta_2}-\limsup t\right)_+,
\qquad
\Delta_2=\frac{\pi}{2\sqrt3},
}
\tag{163.3}
\]

along any Type-II subsequence on which t has a limit. In the same Type-II scope,
Section 125 gives the exact identity

\[
\boxed{Q+u_B=e_{BB}-2k.}
\tag{163.4}
\]

Thus a low-(q-k) regime cannot hide all of its cellwise rhombus boundary demand in
pinned surplus: it must retain a linear loaded-hull residual, either as bounded-face
degree or as repeated/long outer-walk occurrences.

## 163.1 Exact algebra

Section 125's Type-II identities are

\[
e_{BB}=q-m,
\qquad
c_I+s_C=m+k-2,
\tag{163.5}
\]

where m is the number of nonunit sides of the loaded force hull. Section 63 defines

\[
x_B=q-Q-2k,
\qquad
u_B=x_B-m.
\tag{163.6}
\]

Using q=(q-k)+k in (163.6) gives

\[
m=(q-k)-Q-k-u_B.
\tag{163.7}
\]

Substitute (163.7) into the second identity in (163.5):

\[
s_C=(q-k)-Q-u_B-c_I-2.
\tag{163.8}
\]

Therefore

\[
\begin{aligned}
3(h-s_Z)-2s_C
&=3(q-k)-2s_C\\
&=(q-k)+2Q+2u_B+2c_I+4,
\end{aligned}
\]

which proves (163.1). Equation (163.4) follows directly from Section 125's exact
identity e_{BB}=Q+2k+u_B.

## 163.2 Cellwise payment gives the lower tradeoff

The proof-only Section-132 theorem gives

\[
3(h-s_Z)-2s_C
\ge 2\sum_r\sqrt{R_{4,r}}+6,
\tag{163.9}
\]

where R_{4,r} is the number of strict quadrilateral faces in induced harmonic cell r.
In Type II, all but O(D) global strict rhombi are unloaded-only and belong to one of
these cells, so

\[
\sum_rR_{4,r}\ge\Delta_2D^2-o(D^2),
\qquad
\sum_r\sqrt{R_{4,r}}
\ge(\sqrt{\Delta_2}-o(1))D.
\tag{163.10}
\]

Combining (163.1) and (163.9), then dividing by D, gives

\[
t+2\frac{Q+u_B}{D}+o(1)
\ge2\sqrt{\Delta_2}-o(1).
\tag{163.11}
\]

This is the finite-current form of (163.3). The positive-part notation records that
the right side is only informative when the limiting t lies below
2\sqrt{\Delta_2}.

## 163.3 Explicit low-window consequence

For example, on any Type-II subsequence with

\[
\limsup t\le1.85,
\]

the exact certified value 2\sqrt{\Delta_2}=1.9046256137... yields

\[
\liminf\frac{Q+u_B}{D}
\ge0.0273128068\ldots .
\tag{163.12}
\]

More generally, if limsup t<=C_{146}+1/1250, the current audited Section-162 gap gives

\[
\liminf\frac{Q+u_B}{D}
\ge
\frac12\left(2\sqrt{\Delta_2}-1.820313537979\ldots\right)
>0.0421.
\tag{163.13}
\]

This is a conditional low-window bound, not a universal lower bound: t may lie above
2\sqrt{\Delta_2}.

## 163.4 Exact implication chain and limitation

\[
\text{Type-II cellwise rhombus payment}
\to
\text{exact }(q-k)+2(Q+u_B)\text{ decomposition}
\to
\text{linear loaded-boundary residual at low }q-k
\to
\text{candidate target }e_{BB}-2k.
\]

This does not close C-II. It does, however, remove a possible false escape in which a
near-minimal q-k is paid entirely by s_C: the exact Type-II hull ledger forces the
remaining payment into e_{BB}-2k.

The global route-surplus floor from the atomic endpoint theorem sees the global
quantity (b-per(F))+(Q+4f). Section 163 sees Q+u_B=e_{BB}-2k. No inequality between
these two currencies is asserted here; establishing one is the next bridge required
for a contradiction.

**Classification:** proved candidate awaiting independent audit; proof-only Type-II
tradeoff. C-II remains open.
